---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ForeignKeyGenerator from '../../components/ForeignKeyGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="SQL Foreign Key Generator" description="Generate an ALTER TABLE statement adding a foreign key constraint, with ON DELETE behavior.">
  <ForeignKeyGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why ON DELETE behavior deserves a conscious choice</h2>
  <p>A foreign key without a specified ON DELETE behavior defaults to blocking deletion of referenced rows — which surprises developers who expected deleting a parent row to also clean up or nullify related child rows automatically.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter the table, column, and referenced table/column.</li>
    <li>Choose the ON DELETE behavior (CASCADE, SET NULL, RESTRICT).</li>
    <li>Copy the generated ALTER TABLE statement.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p><code>ALTER TABLE orders ADD FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers(id) ON DELETE CASCADE;</code> — deleting a customer automatically deletes their associated orders.</p>

  <h2>Common Foreign Key Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using CASCADE without fully considering the blast radius</strong> — a cascading delete can silently remove far more data than intended if the relationship chain is deep.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming the default behavior is CASCADE</strong> — most databases default to RESTRICT/NO ACTION, blocking the delete entirely unless explicitly overridden.</li>
    <li><strong>Adding a foreign key to a column with existing orphaned values</strong> — the constraint will fail to add until those rows are cleaned up or fixed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Foreign Key Generator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Enforcing referential integrity between related tables</li>
    <li>Deciding whether deleting a parent record should cascade, nullify, or block child records</li>
    <li>Adding a missing foreign key constraint to an existing schema</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>SQL Foreign Key FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What's the safest default ON DELETE behavior?</strong> RESTRICT (blocking the delete) is the safest default when unsure — it forces an explicit decision rather than silently cascading or nullifying data.</p>
</ToolLayout>
